  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4 print:hidden">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span>Roofing Squares for 2,000 Sq Ft Roof</span>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">How Many Squares of Shingles Do I Need for a 2,000 Sq Ft Roof?</h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <div class="p-5 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-950 mt-0 mb-2">⚡ Quick Answer Summary:</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 text-base leading-relaxed mb-0">
          For a <strong>2,000 sq ft ground footprint home</strong> with a standard <strong>6/12 pitch roof</strong>, you need approximately <strong>25 squares of shingles (75 bundles total)</strong>. This accounts for roof slope expansion (+11.8%), 1-foot eave overhangs, and a standard <strong>10% waste factor</strong> for starter strips, hips, valleys, and ridge caps.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Roofing materials are not sold by the individual shingle or square foot. Instead, roofing contractors and suppliers measure roofs in <strong>"Squares."</strong> Understanding how to convert your home's square footage into squares and shingle bundles prevents under-ordering or over-paying.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-950 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Manual Math: Use Our Free Roofing Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 mb-4">Enter your roof area and pitch ratio to instantly calculate exact squares, bundle counts, underlayment rolls, and RSMeans DIY vs. contractor costs.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/asphalt-shingles" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to the Asphalt Shingles Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. What is a "Roofing Square"?</h2>
      <p>
        In construction, <strong>1 Roofing Square = 100 square feet of roof surface area</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><strong>1 Square:</strong> 100 sq ft of coverage</li>
        <li><strong>1 Bundle of Shingles:</strong> Covers ~33.3 sq ft</li>
        <li><strong>3 Bundles:</strong> Equal exactly 1 Square (100 sq ft)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. Why a 2,000 Sq Ft House Needs MORE Than 20 Squares</h2>
      <p>
        A common rookie mistake is dividing a home's 2,000 sq ft floor area by 100 to get 20 squares. If you buy 20 squares (60 bundles), you will run out of shingles halfway through your roof. Here are the 3 reasons why:
      </p>
      
      <ol class="space-y-4">
        <li>
          <strong>Roof Pitch (Slope Expansion):</strong> A pitched roof has more surface area than a flat ground footprint. The steeper your roof slope, the more square feet of shingles required.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Eaves &amp; Gable Overhangs:</strong> Roof rafters extend past exterior walls by 12 to 24 inches on all sides. A 2,000 sq ft house usually has a 2,200+ sq ft roof footprint.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Waste Factor &amp; Trim:</strong> Cutting shingles around valleys, dormers, chimneys, and starter strips produces mandatory scrap.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. Roof Pitch Multiplier Table</h2>
      <p>
        Multiply your ground footprint by the pitch multiplier below to find actual roof surface area:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-100 border-b border-gray-200">
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Roof Pitch (Rise / Run)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Slope Multiplier</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">2,000 Sq Ft Footprint Surface Area</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
            <tr><td class="p-3">3/12 (Low Slope)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.031</td><td class="p-3">2,062 sq ft (20.6 Sq)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3">4/12 (Standard Walkable)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.054</td><td class="p-3">2,108 sq ft (21.1 Sq)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50 font-medium"><td class="p-3">6/12 (Common Residential)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.118</td><td class="p-3">2,236 sq ft (22.4 Sq)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3">8/12 (Steep Slope)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.202</td><td class="p-3">2,404 sq ft (24.0 Sq)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3">10/12 (Very Steep)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.302</td><td class="p-3">2,604 sq ft (26.0 Sq)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3">12/12 (45° Angle)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">1.414</td><td class="p-3">2,828 sq ft (28.3 Sq)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">4. Step-by-Step Calculation Example</h2>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 space-y-3 font-mono text-sm">
        <p><strong>Step 1: Ground Footprint + Overhangs</strong> = 2,200 sq ft</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2: Apply 6/12 Pitch Multiplier (1.118)</strong> = 2,200 × 1.118 = 2,460 sq ft</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3: Add 10% Waste Factor (1.10)</strong> = 2,460 × 1.10 = 2,706 sq ft</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4: Convert to Squares</strong> = 2,706 / 100 = <strong>27.1 Squares</strong></p>
        <p><strong>Step 5: Convert to Bundles (× 3)</strong> = 27.1 × 3 = <strong>82 Bundles</strong></p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">5. What Else Do You Need besides Shingles?</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Underlayment Rolls:</strong> Synthetic felt rolls usually cover 1,000 sq ft each (2–3 rolls needed for a 2,000 sq ft home).</li>
        <li><strong>Starter Strip Shingles:</strong> Required along all eaves and gables to prevent wind uplift.</li>
        <li><strong>Hip &amp; Ridge Cap Shingles:</strong> 1 bundle covers ~25 to 35 linear feet of ridge line.</li>
        <li><strong>Roofing Nails:</strong> 4 to 6 coils of 1-1/4" ring-shank galvanized nails per square.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Bottom Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 p-6 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl text-center">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Ready to Calculate Your Exact Roof Materials?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-4">Use our free asphalt shingles calculator to get instant bundle counts, synthetic underlayment, starter strips, and DIY vs contractor cost benchmarks.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/asphalt-shingles" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-8 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Open Asphalt Shingles Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
